PASS level_intents: all 120 levels have a specific intent
PASS no_filler_levels: no trivial filler levels after the tutorial
FAIL level_design: level-design review 7/10 (bar 8), 4 level(s) below 6/10: Station Zen has a strong, readable core idea: seats = 6x(1+carriages)x36/(2 hops), so players learn to count line length. Every level ships with a worked solution and a named trap. Each city adds one clear mechanic: tunnels, carriages, hills, ferries, snow, markets, interchanges, loops, waves and heritage stations. Boards are small and clean. The calm pillars (a forecast, no surprise fails, trap rate 0) are well supported. Casual win rate across the last third averages about 62%, which meets the studio target. Skilled rates are mostly 82-99%, also within target.

The main weakness is repetition. Roughly two thirds of the levels use the same answer: split one long line into short lines, and the trap is the one long line. The carriage levels (L26-29, 33, 34, 40, 42, 53, 67, 68, 72) repeat 'put it on the line the crowd must ride, not the busy-looking one'. The late-station levels repeat 'end the line where the new stop will open'. The ring levels (L85, 86, 91, 94, 116) are near clones. Several intents don't match the board. L16 is billed as the first weekly card, but L3 already had one. L73-75 and L79 teach 'interchange' with pre-placed interchanges, so the upgrade move is never taught alone before it appears combined in L78/80/84. L109 lists loops but doesn't use them. Several 'gentle send-off' mastery levels are not gentle (L120 at 58%, L96 at 63%). Several 3rd-star goals are trivial or odd (L75 '0 tracks to spare', L92). Bosses and finales marked 'trap rate ?' and 'optimal > search cap' (L6, 11, 12, 35, 48, 54, 59, 62, 83, 102, 103) can't be fully checked for hidden fatal moves. L84 (casual 73%, skilled 76%) shows luck or guessing. L3 (99% casual) makes the first card pick meaningless. Overall this is a solid, kind, well-signposted set. It would reach top-tier with fewer clones, one distinct aha per level, and honest mechanic labelling. Curve: Onboarding is good: L1-5 teach lines, a transfer, line length, card and construction-site ideas, one at a time, with ≥85% casual wins. City 2 (tunnels, L13-24) and City 3 (carriages, L25-36) each have a clear teach, practise, twist, combine, boss, breather, mastery rhythm. Breathers come after bosses (L7, 19, 31, 43, 55, 67, 79, 91, 103, 115), and chapter finales test the city's idea. Casual win rates fall steadily from the high 80s to about 50-65%, with no run of three easy levels. Problems: (1) The set is long, and from about L40 the 'short line beats long line, carriage on the non-obvious line' insight recurs, so players can learn the trick and stop reading boards. Several levels need a new verb, not new numbers. (2) Mechanics bundle too fast late: L58, 59 and 118 stack 5-6 systems, so the aha is buried. (3) Interchange upgrade is never taught alone; it first appears in a card pick (L78) or combined (L80, 84). Loops (L85-91) are four near-identical 'close the ring' boards before the one-way rail adds anything. (4) Hard levels cluster in the final 40: 15+ levels have casual win rates of 47-58%, with no breather in between that is truly easier. (5) The 'gentle send-off' mastery levels (L84, 96, 108, 120) are not gentler than their bosses. (6) The first weekly card is mislabelled at L16, and the L1-3 'overcrowding off' text conflicts with traps that lose by full rings. Suggestion: cut or merge about 15 clone levels, move the interchange tutorial to its own early level, and add a few 'no-trap' sandbox boards to vary the rhythm.
REDESIGN THESE LEVELS (keep the ones not listed):
L75 (5/10): The 3rd star 'finish with 0 tracks to spare' is the same as winning. The decision between E and D is hard to tell apart from L73. → Give 4 tracks and make the 3rd star 'one spare'. Make D's demand grow so skipping it is a real call.
L84 (5/10): Skilled 76% against casual 73% shows luck or guessing. There are 11 start choices. It is not a gentle send-off. → Cut the choices to about 6 and put E's forecast ring on the first screen. Make the interchange the only way to win, or remove the stub.
L86 (5/10): A near-clone of L85. The outside ring is the obvious choice, and the decoy rail adds a small twist. → Merge it with L85, or put a late station on the middle rung so the decoy becomes tempting.
L94 (5/10): The answer is forced (the whole board is a ring). It is a copy of L85 and L91 with snow, and the 11 moves are busywork. → Add a decoy rung that closes a short loop without covering all stops, plus a late station.
L3 (6/10): 99% casual with both cards winning means neither the split nor the card is a real decision. It also bundles two ideas. The trap is described as 'full wk4' while overcrowding is off. → Move the card to L5 or make the +1 line card miss the goal so the pick matters. Align the trap with the goal rule (it loses on deliveries), not on rings.
L10 (6/10): Another 'where do I split the line' board, same idea as L3 and L5. The bend at D adds little. → Replace it with a board where the right split point moves when a late station opens, or fold it into L5.
L16 (6/10): Billed as 'the first weekly card' but L3 had one. The tunnel card looks like a fatal pick that only shows up weeks later. → Relabel it as the first tunnel card. Let the tunnel card still win but lose the 3rd star, so it teaches and does not trap.
L73 (6/10): The interchange is pre-placed. The player never uses the upgrade move, so this teaches 'who waits', not interchange. → Let the player place one interchange upgrade on a quiet stop. Relabel the lesson 'let a robust stop wait'.
L91 (6/10): A fourth 'close the ring' board. It is pretty but not a new idea. → Make it a one-way-rail ring or a loop with a stub, so the breather isn't a repeat.
L116 (6/10): A re-skin of the L86/L87 ring with a decoy rung. → Replace it, or add a late station that decides which loop wins.
L1 (7/10):  → 
L6 (7/10): 79% casual, with the aha text calling the goal tight. It reads as a mid-level, not a boss. The trap rate is unknown. → Raise the goal to about 160 so a lopsided split misses it. Or add a week-4 rush so it feels like a test.
L9 (7/10): Close to L2 (a hub with two lines). The twist is not clearly different, and L9 mostly asks the player to see a plus. → Make hub demand lopsided so only one pairing of arms (A-C-D plus B-C-E vs A-C-B plus D-C-E) clears the goal.
L14 (7/10): Near-repeat of L13 (pick one of two crossings). → Give three piers and two crossing points, with only one that lets two lines cover both banks.
L17 (7/10): Same 'short lines run often with feeders' idea as L3, L13 and L15. → Make the two tunnels force a three-line hub. The feeders carry no riders alone, so the player must see why.
L32 (7/10): Same shape as L16: a card that looks right but is wrong because the late station needs track. → Make the carriage card the better pick here and the tracks card the trap, to break the pattern.
L33 (7/10): Clone of the carriage-on-the-obvious-line trap from L26 and L29. Long empty corridors read as dead space. → Shorten the lane. Or make the busy stop's demand shift in week 3 so the right carriage line changes.
L34 (7/10): Repeats the L18 and L28 'scarce tunnel' logic with a carriage added. → Give the carriage a real second-best use, so players must count seats on both candidate lines.
L36 (7/10): Near-clone of L30 (late stop, split at B, carriage on the long line). A 'gentle' send-off at 7 weeks and 10 moves is long. → Shorten it, or give it a one-line unique twist, such as a carriage-less alternative that scrapes a win.
L38 (7/10): Same lesson as L5 and L20 (keep the slow part off the busy line). → Add a second hill option so the player must compare two climbs.
L41 (7/10): Close in spirit to L39 (a hill isn't always the slow way). → Merge it with L39, or add a station on the climb that makes the detour tempting.
L43 (7/10): The hill is a pure decoy. Little insight, but it is fine as a rest. → Make the one-line star require a Hamiltonian choice, as in L7.
L44 (7/10): A repeat of the L16/L18 late-stop pattern with hills. → Add a hill option that does win, so it is a trade-off and not a trap.
L47 (7/10): The aha is a mouthful. Three traps (steep spur, top road, carriage) blur it. → Remove the steep-road option, or drop the carriage so there is one clear decision.
L48 (7/10): Repeats the 'card that looks right is wrong' pattern from L16 and L32. Not gentle. Trap rate unknown. → Make it a clean recap with a calm card as an equally valid pick.

level-design score 7/10 — Station Zen has a strong, readable core idea: seats = 6x(1+carriages)x36/(2 hops), so players learn to count line length. Every level ships with a worked solution and a named trap. Each city adds one clear mechanic: tunnels, carriages, hills, ferries, snow, markets, interchanges, loops, waves and heritage stations. Boards are small and clean. The calm pillars (a forecast, no surprise fails, trap rate 0) are well supported. Casual win rate across the last third averages about 62%, which meets the studio target. Skilled rates are mostly 82-99%, also within target.

The main weakness is repetition. Roughly two thirds of the levels use the same answer: split one long line into short lines, and the trap is the one long line. The carriage levels (L26-29, 33, 34, 40, 42, 53, 67, 68, 72) repeat 'put it on the line the crowd must ride, not the busy-looking one'. The late-station levels repeat 'end the line where the new stop will open'. The ring levels (L85, 86, 91, 94, 116) are near clones. Several intents don't match the board. L16 is billed as the first weekly card, but L3 already had one. L73-75 and L79 teach 'interchange' with pre-placed interchanges, so the upgrade move is never taught alone before it appears combined in L78/80/84. L109 lists loops but doesn't use them. Several 'gentle send-off' mastery levels are not gentle (L120 at 58%, L96 at 63%). Several 3rd-star goals are trivial or odd (L75 '0 tracks to spare', L92). Bosses and finales marked 'trap rate ?' and 'optimal > search cap' (L6, 11, 12, 35, 48, 54, 59, 62, 83, 102, 103) can't be fully checked for hidden fatal moves. L84 (casual 73%, skilled 76%) shows luck or guessing. L3 (99% casual) makes the first card pick meaningless. Overall this is a solid, kind, well-signposted set. It would reach top-tier with fewer clones, one distinct aha per level, and honest mechanic labelling.
curve: Onboarding is good: L1-5 teach lines, a transfer, line length, card and construction-site ideas, one at a time, with ≥85% casual wins. City 2 (tunnels, L13-24) and City 3 (carriages, L25-36) each have a clear teach, practise, twist, combine, boss, breather, mastery rhythm. Breathers come after bosses (L7, 19, 31, 43, 55, 67, 79, 91, 103, 115), and chapter finales test the city's idea. Casual win rates fall steadily from the high 80s to about 50-65%, with no run of three easy levels. Problems: (1) The set is long, and from about L40 the 'short line beats long line, carriage on the non-obvious line' insight recurs, so players can learn the trick and stop reading boards. Several levels need a new verb, not new numbers. (2) Mechanics bundle too fast late: L58, 59 and 118 stack 5-6 systems, so the aha is buried. (3) Interchange upgrade is never taught alone; it first appears in a card pick (L78) or combined (L80, 84). Loops (L85-91) are four near-identical 'close the ring' boards before the one-way rail adds anything. (4) Hard levels cluster in the final 40: 15+ levels have casual win rates of 47-58%, with no breather in between that is truly easier. (5) The 'gentle send-off' mastery levels (L84, 96, 108, 120) are not gentler than their bosses. (6) The first weekly card is mislabelled at L16, and the L1-3 'overcrowding off' text conflicts with traps that lose by full rings. Suggestion: cut or merge about 15 clone levels, move the interchange tutorial to its own early level, and add a few 'no-trap' sandbox boards to vary the rhythm.
